{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>הכל.</b> הכל יבא להם — as will come to all. One fate for the righteous and for the wicked; there is no difference between them. To the good and to the pure and to the impure: here 'impure' is the opposite of 'good and pure,' just as there is פקח (seeing) in the verse 'who gives a mouth to a man' — the opposite of blind — and 'the hearer,' as in 'opens blind eyes, unseals deaf ears.' The grammatical analysis of כטוב כחוטא: the prefix כ in the sacred language functions like כעבד כאדניו, for it makes the slave like the master and the master like the slave — no difference between them. Similarly כמוני כמוך, כעמי כעמך, כחשכה כאורה. And the one who explains כימים אשר ישבתם — 'the days' has no sense or flavor in this explanation, for what does 'you dwelt' have to do with 'Kadesh days, many days, like the days that you dwelt'? The correct reading is that from Kadesh the spies went out, and in the fortieth year they came to Kadesh the city; and all the years they were in the wilderness of Kadesh, and this is its meaning: 'and you dwelt in Kadesh many days, like the days that you dwelt there, until the return of the scouts' — as in 'according to the number of the days that you scouted the land.' For when the spies arrived, they said 'Here we are; let us go up to the place.' It is possible that צדיק ורשע refers to his deeds, טמא וטהור to his body, וזובח to his property, וטוב וחוטא to his heart, ונשבע to his speech."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "The text includes a long digression on כימים אשר ישבתם (Deut. 1:46) interrupting the grammatical discussion of Ecclesiastes; translated as it stands. The phrase 'והמפרש' (and the one who explains) likely refers to a specific commentator, unidentified here."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:28:42.773090323Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}